---
/**
 * How a commune page picks the figures that stand out and what it sets beside each. Every
 * sentence here has to stay true of insights/src/: detect.ts, filter.ts, context.ts and
 * run.ts, and of api/src/lib/mismatch.ts. Their numbers come from `method`, read from that
 * code, and site/tests/insights.test.ts fails on any other number typed here. The mirror of
 * this page under /fr/ says the same in French.
 */
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import { REPO_URL, t } from "../../i18n/ui";
import { numbers, percent } from "../../lib/format";
import { method } from "../../lib/insights";

const locale = "en";
const copy = t(locale);

const toc = [
  { id: "stands-out", label: "What stands out" },
  { id: "left-out", label: "What’s left out" },
  { id: "context", label: "What’s beside each figure" },
  { id: "no-model", label: "No model" },
  { id: "pilot", label: "The earlier design" },
  { id: "sources", label: "Where it lives" },
];

const n = numbers(locale);
const count = (value: number) => n.format(value);
const whole = (fraction: number) => percent(locale, fraction * 100, { digits: 0 });
const pct = (value: number) => percent(locale, value, { digits: 0 });

const file = (path: string) => `${REPO_URL}/blob/main/${path}`;
---

<Docs
  locale={locale}
  route="docs/insights/"
  title="How the standout figures are picked"
  description="How a commune page picks the figures that stand out, what it leaves out, and where each number beside them comes from."
  lede="A commune page can show the figures where it stands furthest from others, with the communes it borders and 2014 beside each one. This is how they’re picked and where every number comes from. No language model writes any of it."
  toc={toc}
>
  <p class="note"><a href="/insights/">{copy.insightsFigures}</a></p>
  <section id="stands-out">
    <h2>What stands out</h2>
    <p>
      A commune of {count(method.extremeFloor)} people or more stands out when it’s in the top or bottom{" "}
      {whole(method.extremeTail)} of those communes on a measure. A unit stands out when its figure moved since 2014
      by {count(method.deviations)} standard deviations or more from the average change across its level, for a
      level of {count(method.changeLevelSize)} units or more and a measure the 2014 census asked the same way. A
      commune or a province also stands out when its gap from its province’s or région’s figure is{" "}
      {count(method.deviations)} standard deviations or more from the average gap under that parent, where the
      parent holds {count(method.gapGroupSize)} units or more. Changes and gaps count only for communes of{" "}
      {count(method.changeGapFloor)} people or more, where a few households can’t swing a share on their own.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every measure is a rate, a share or an average, since a raw count would pick out a big place for being big.
      A commune matched to 2014 through the crosswalk has no change to compare, since its 2014 figure covers other
      ground. The {count(method.kept)} figures that stand out most are kept, at most {count(method.perPlace)} for
      any one place. Only a commune’s are published, since a commune page is where they show.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="left-out">
    <h2>What’s left out</h2>
    <p>
      A possible error in the data is left out. The languages people speak, read and write, education and
      illiteracy move slowly. When one of them swings in a place whose population moved by less than{" "}
      {whole(method.artefactCeiling)}, and no neighbour moved the same way by at least half as much, the figure
      may be an error in the data. So may every figure in a record that can’t be right, such as more than{" "}
      {count(method.peoplePerRoom)} people per room or a household of more than {count(method.householdSize)}.
    </p>
    <p>
      So is a commune with any figure the two censuses disagree on. For a local language, that’s a share that moved
      more than {count(method.swing)} points between 2014 and 2024, either way. For running water, electricity, the
      public sewer, a toilet and a kitchen, it’s a share that fell more than {count(method.fall)} points. Either
      way, the move has to be larger than the population itself changed, in percent. Both figures are HCP’s, as
      published. The two censuses disagree, so the commune isn’t set against a neighbour, and it has no section here.
    </p>
    <p>
      The shares of men and women are left out too, since several southern communes count special populations.
      Getting to work by tram, train, bus, taxi or transport laid on by an employer is left out, since those
      services only some places have. Where a list of private terms is set up, a figure whose line uses one is
      held back.
    </p>
    <p>
      An economy or housing figure is left out when the commune has fewer than {count(method.smallBase)} businesses,
      or fewer than {count(method.smallBase)} urban dwellings. A handful of them can swing a share.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="context">
    <h2>What’s beside each figure</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>The commune’s other figures.</strong> A commune’s figures that stand out are shown together, and
      each one’s record in the API lists the others.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The communes it borders.</strong> Two communes border each other when their OpenStreetMap boundaries
      share a segment. Beside each figure is the median of the same figure across those communes, and the one
      furthest from this commune’s own. A neighbour of fewer than {count(method.neighbourFloor)} people is left
      out, and so is one with any figure the two censuses disagree on. For a change since 2014, their change is
      compared, and a neighbour matched to 2014 through the crosswalk is left out.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>2014 and Morocco.</strong> Where the 2014 census asked the figure the same way, the page gives the
      commune’s 2014 figure and how Morocco’s moved, in whole points for a share. It doesn’t for a commune
      matched through the crosswalk.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>An estimate from a sample.</strong> In a commune of {count(method.sampleHouseholds)} households or more,
      the long questionnaire went to a sample of households. A census figure there carries a note that it’s an estimate,
      linking to <a href="/docs/indicators/#reading">how the census was read</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Communes of the same kind.</strong> A census figure is also set against communes of the same type,
      urban or rural, and the same size: under {count(method.peerSmall)} people, {count(method.peerSmall)} to{" "}
      {count(method.peerMid)}, or over {count(method.peerMid)}. If it’s in the top {pct(method.peerTop)},{" "}
      {pct(method.peerHigh)} or {pct(method.peerWide)} of a group of at least {count(method.peerGroup)} communes,
      a line says so. Economy and housing figures aren’t compared this way.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="no-model">
    <h2>No model</h2>
    <p>
      Every line is a fixed sentence in English and French with the numbers put in, and every number is HCP’s or
      worked out from HCP’s. <code>pnpm insights</code> builds it all from the dataset with no model and no network
      call, and the same dataset always gives the same files.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="pilot">
    <h2>The earlier design</h2>
    <p>
      An earlier design had a language model propose possible reasons for each figure, and a second model argue
      against them. The 2026 pilot that chose its models is published as a study, with its preregistration, ratings
      and results, in <a href={file("insights/pilot/README.md")}><code>insights/pilot/</code></a>. The full run
      that followed left nearly half the figures with no reason that held, and whether a reason was sound couldn’t
      be graded reliably. So this version shows the figures with their context instead.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="sources">
    <h2>Where it lives</h2>
    <p>
      The pipeline is in <a href={file("insights/")}><code>insights/</code></a>, and what it publishes is in{" "}
      <a href={file("data/v1/insights/")}><code>data/v1/insights/</code></a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
